1
00:00:02,679 --> 00:00:09,578
CHAPTER 8 The Queen’s croquet ground A large rose tree stood near the

2
00:00:09,579 --> 00:00:14,559
entrance of the garden: the roses growing on it were white, but there were three

3
00:00:14,560 --> 00:00:17,559
gardeners at it, busily painting them red.

4
00:00:17,560 --> 00:00:23,078
Alice thought this a very curious thing, and she went near to watch them, and just

5
00:00:23,079 --> 00:00:26,197
as she came up to them she heard one of them say,

6
00:00:26,198 --> 00:00:31,538
“Look out now, Five! Don’t go splash and paint over me like that!” “I couldn’t help

7
00:00:31,539 --> 00:00:37,358
it,” said Five, in a silky tone; “Seven jogged my elbow.” On which Seven looked up

8
00:00:37,359 --> 00:00:41,978
and said, “That’s right, Five! Always lay the blame on others!”

9
00:00:41,979 --> 00:00:47,379
You’d better not talk!” said Five. “I heard the Queen say only yesterday you deserve

10
00:00:47,380 --> 00:00:52,197
to be beheaded!” “What for?” said the one who had spoken up first.

11
00:00:52,198 --> 00:00:56,318
“That’s none of your business too said Seven.

12
00:00:56,319 --> 00:01:01,657
“Yes, it is his business!” said Five, “and I’ll tell him— —it was for bringing the

13
00:01:01,658 --> 00:01:07,559
cook tulip Roots instead of onions.” Seven flung down his brush, and had just begun

14
00:01:07,560 --> 00:01:14,318
“Well all of the unjust things—” when is I chanced to fall upon Alice,

15
00:01:14,319 --> 00:01:18,818
as she stood watching them, and he checked himself suddenly: the others

16
00:01:18,819 --> 00:01:22,959
looked round also, and all of them bowed low.

17
00:01:22,960 --> 00:01:29,499
“Would you tell me,” said Alice, a little timidly, “why you are painting those roses?”

18
00:01:29,500 --> 00:01:35,919
Five and Seven said nothing, but looked at Two. Two began in a low voice Roy the

19
00:01:35,920 --> 00:01:38,978
fact is, you see, Miss, this here ought to

20
00:01:38,979 --> 00:01:41,678
have been a red rose tree and we put in

21
00:01:41,679 --> 00:01:46,778
a white one by mistake; and if the Queen was to find out, we should all have our

22
00:01:46,779 --> 00:01:52,898
heads cut off, you know. So you see, Miss, we’re doing our best before she comes, to—”

23
00:01:52,899 --> 00:01:56,678
At this moment Five, who had been anxiously looking around the garden,

24
00:01:56,679 --> 00:02:02,978
called out Queen! The Queen!” and the three gardeners instantly threw

25
00:02:02,979 --> 00:02:05,858
themselves flat upon their faces.

26
00:02:05,859 --> 00:02:12,099
There is a sound of many footsteps, and Alice looked round, eager to see the Queen.

27
00:02:12,100 --> 00:02:16,778
First came ten soldiers carrying clubs; these were all shaped like the three

28
00:02:16,779 --> 00:02:21,818
gardeners, oblong and flat, with their hands and feet at the corners:

29
00:02:21,819 --> 00:02:27,578
next the ten courtiers; these were ornamented all over with diamonds, and

30
00:02:27,579 --> 00:02:30,099
walked two and two, as the soldiers did.

31
00:02:30,100 --> 00:02:34,657
After these came the royal children; there were 10 of them, and the little

32
00:02:34,658 --> 00:02:40,237
deers came jumping merrily along hand in hand, in couples: they were all ornamented

33
00:02:40,238 --> 00:02:46,178
with hearts. Next came the guests, mostly Kings and Queens, and among them Alice

34
00:02:46,179 --> 00:02:50,999
recognized the White Rabbit: it was talking in a hurried nervous manner,

35
00:02:51,000 --> 00:02:56,318
smiling at everything that was said, and went by without noticing her. Then

36
00:02:56,319 --> 00:03:01,118
followed the Knave of Hearts, carrying the King’s crown on a crimson velvet

37
00:03:01,119 --> 00:03:07,478
cushion; and, last of all this grand procession, came THE KING AND QUEEN OF

38
00:03:07,479 --> 00:03:11,858
HEARTS. Alice was rather doubtful whether she ought not to lie down on her face

39
00:03:11,859 --> 00:03:15,999
like the three gardeners, but she could not remember ever having heard of such a

40
00:03:16,000 --> 00:03:21,339
rule at processions; “and besides, what would be the use of a procession,” thought

41
00:03:21,340 --> 00:03:26,978
she, “if people had all to lie down upon their faces, so they couldn’t see it?”

42
00:03:26,979 --> 00:03:32,678
So she stood still where she was, and waited. When the procession came opposite

43
00:03:32,679 --> 00:03:41,978
to Alice, they all stopped and looked at her, and the Queen said severely “Who is this?”

44
00:03:41,979 --> 00:03:47,559
She said it to the Knave of Hearts, who only bowed and smiled in reply. “Idiot!”

45
00:03:47,560 --> 00:03:52,538
said the Queen, tossing her head impatiently; and, turning to Alice, she

46
00:03:52,539 --> 00:03:58,959
went on, “What’s your name, child?” “My name is Alice, so it pleased your

47
00:03:58,960 --> 00:04:04,718
Majesty,” said Alice very politely; but she added, to herself, “Why, they’re only a pack

48
00:04:04,719 --> 00:04:11,638
of cards, after all. I needn’t be afraid of them!” “And who are these? said the Queen,

49
00:04:11,639 --> 00:04:18,157
pointing to the three gardeners who were lying round the rose tree for, you see, as

50
00:04:18,158 --> 00:04:23,078
they were lying on their faces, and the pattern on their backs was the same as

51
00:04:23,079 --> 00:04:26,559
the rest of the pack, she could not tell whether they were

52
00:04:26,560 --> 00:04:31,178
gardeners, or soldiers, or courtiers, or three of her own children.

53
00:04:31,179 --> 00:04:37,178
“How should I know?” said Alice, surprised at her own courage. “It’s no business of mine

54
00:04:37,179 --> 00:04:42,879
The Queen turned crimson with fury, and, after glaring at her for a moment like a

55
00:04:42,880 --> 00:04:46,419
wild beast, screamed “Off with her head!

56
00:04:46,420 --> 00:04:55,538
Off—” “Nonsense!” said Alice, very loudly and decidedly, and the Queen was silent.

57
00:04:55,539 --> 00:05:01,959
The King laid his hand upon her arm, and timidly said “Consider, my dear: she is

58
00:05:01,960 --> 00:05:09,157
only a child!” The Queen turned angrily away from him, and said to the Knave “Turn them over!”

59
00:05:09,158 --> 00:05:13,559
The Nave did so, very carefully, with one foot.

60
00:05:13,560 --> 00:05:21,818
“Get up!” said the Queen, in a shrill, loud voice, and the three gardeners instantly jumped

61
00:05:21,819 --> 00:05:27,398
up, and began bowing to the King, the Queen, the royal children, and everybody

62
00:05:27,399 --> 00:05:31,299
else. “Leave off that!” screamed the Queen.

63
00:05:31,300 --> 00:05:37,678
“You make me giddy.” And then, turning to the Rose Tree she went on,

64
00:05:37,679 --> 00:05:44,678
“What have you been doing here?” “May please your Majesty,” said Two, in a very humble

65
00:05:44,679 --> 00:05:51,519
tone, going down on one knee as he spoke, “we were trying—” I see!” said the Queen, who

66
00:05:51,520 --> 00:05:54,398
had meanwhile been examining the roses.

67
00:05:54,399 --> 00:06:00,697
“Off with their heads!” and the procession moved on, three of the

68
00:06:00,698 --> 00:06:07,178
soldiers remaining behind to execute the unfortunate gardeners, who ran to Alice for protection.

69
00:06:07,179 --> 00:06:12,157
“You shan’t be beheaded!” said Alice as she put them into a large flower pot that

70
00:06:12,158 --> 00:06:13,178
stood near.

71
00:06:13,179 --> 00:06:17,678
The three soldiers wandered about for a minute or two, looking for them, and then

72
00:06:17,679 --> 00:06:20,559
quietly marched off after the others.

73
00:06:20,560 --> 00:06:23,999
“Are their heads off?” shouted the Queen.

74
00:06:24,000 --> 00:06:30,339
“Their heads are gone they may please your Majesty!” the soldiers shouted in reply.

75
00:06:30,340 --> 00:06:34,538
“That’s right!” shouted the Queen.

76
00:06:34,539 --> 00:06:38,978
“Can you play croquet?” The soldiers were silent, and looked at

77
00:06:38,979 --> 00:06:42,879
Alice, as the question was evidently meant for her.

78
00:06:42,880 --> 00:06:49,358
“Yes!” shouted Alice. “Come on, then!” roared the Queen, and Alice joined the

79
00:06:49,359 --> 00:06:56,078
procession, wondering very much what would happen next. “It’s— —it’s a very fine day!”

80
00:06:56,079 --> 00:07:01,419
said a timid voice at her side. She was walking by the White Rabbit, who was

81
00:07:01,420 --> 00:07:09,578
peeping anxiously into her face. “Very,” said Alice: “—where’s the Duchess?” “Hush! Hush!”

82
00:07:09,579 --> 00:07:12,578
said the Rabbit in a low, hurried tone.

83
00:07:12,579 --> 00:07:17,078
He looked anxiously over his shoulder as he spoke, and then raised himself upon

84
00:07:17,079 --> 00:07:24,459
tiptoe, put his mouth close to her ear, and whispered “She’s under sentence of execution.” “What

85
00:07:24,460 --> 00:07:27,038
for?” said Alice.

86
00:07:27,039 --> 00:07:33,578
“Did you say ‘What a pity!’?” the Rabbit asked. “No, I didn’t,” said Alice:

87
00:07:33,579 --> 00:07:38,138
“I don’t think it’s at all a pity. I said ‘What for?’”

88
00:07:38,139 --> 00:07:42,578
“She boxed the Queen’s ears—” the Rabbit began.

89
00:07:42,579 --> 00:07:44,617
Alice gave a little scream of laughter.

90
00:07:44,618 --> 00:07:50,617
“Oh, hush!” the Rabbit whispered in a frightened tone. “The Queen will hear you do You see,

91
00:07:50,618 --> 00:07:56,919
she came rather late, and the Queen said—” “Get to your places!” shouted the Queen in

92
00:07:56,920 --> 00:08:01,718
a voice of thunder, and people began running about in all directions, tumbling

93
00:08:01,719 --> 00:08:08,499
up against each other; however, they got settled down in a minute or two, and the game began.

94
00:08:08,500 --> 00:08:12,879
Alice thought she had never seen such a curious croquet ground in all her life;

95
00:08:12,880 --> 00:08:19,419
it was all ridges and furrows and the balls were live hedgehogs, the mallets

96
00:08:19,420 --> 00:08:24,459
live flamingos and the soldiers had to double themselves up to stand on their

97
00:08:24,460 --> 00:08:27,157
hands and feet, to make the arches.

98
00:08:27,158 --> 00:08:31,718
The chief difficulty Alice found at first was in managing her flamingo:

99
00:08:31,719 --> 00:08:35,858
she succeeded in getting its body tucked away, comfortably enough, under her arm,

100
00:08:35,859 --> 00:08:40,898
with its legs hanging down, but generally, just as she had got its neck nicely

101
00:08:40,899 --> 00:08:44,737
straightened out, and was going to give the hedgehog a blow with its head, it

102
00:08:44,738 --> 00:08:49,059
would twist itself around and look up in her face, with such a puzzled expression

103
00:08:49,060 --> 00:08:53,138
that she could not help bursting out laughing: and when she had got its head

104
00:08:53,139 --> 00:08:57,398
down, and was going to begin again, it was very provoking to find that the hedgehog

105
00:08:57,399 --> 00:09:01,419
had unrolled itself, and was in the act of crawling away:

106
00:09:01,420 --> 00:09:06,157
besides all this, there was generally a ridge or furrow in the way wherever she

107
00:09:06,158 --> 00:09:09,938
wanted to send the hedgehog to, and, as the doubled up soldiers were always

108
00:09:09,939 --> 00:09:14,499
getting up and walking off to other parts of the ground, Alice soon came to

109
00:09:14,500 --> 00:09:18,938
the conclusion that it was a very difficult game indeed. The players all

110
00:09:18,939 --> 00:09:23,259
played at once without waiting for turns quarreling all the while, and fighting

111
00:09:23,260 --> 00:09:29,138
for the hedgehogs; and in a very short time the Queen was in a furious passion,

112
00:09:29,139 --> 00:09:36,759
and went stamping about, and shouting “Off with his head!” or “Off with her head!” about

113
00:09:36,760 --> 00:09:38,197
once in a minute.

114
00:09:38,198 --> 00:09:43,839
Alice began to feel very uneasy: to be sure, she had not as yet had any dispute

115
00:09:43,840 --> 00:09:48,818
with the Queen, but she knew that it might happen any minute, “and then,” thought

116
00:09:48,819 --> 00:09:53,858
she, “what would become of me? They’re dreadfully fond of beheading people here;

117
00:09:53,859 --> 00:09:59,617
the great wonder is, that there’s anyone left alive!” She was looking about for

118
00:09:59,618 --> 00:10:04,538
some way of escape, and wondering whether she could get away without being seen,

119
00:10:04,539 --> 00:10:09,879
when she noticed a curious appearance in the air: it puzzled her very much at

120
00:10:09,880 --> 00:10:12,398
first, but, after watching it a minute or

121
00:10:12,399 --> 00:10:15,999
two, she made it out to be a grin, and she

122
00:10:16,000 --> 00:10:22,237
said to herself oh the Cheshire Cat: now I shall have somebody to talk to.”

123
00:10:22,238 --> 00:10:26,919
“How are you getting on?” said the Cat, as soon as there was mouth enough for it to

124
00:10:26,920 --> 00:10:27,879
speak with.

125
00:10:27,880 --> 00:10:33,278
Alice waited till the eyes appeared, and then nodded. “It’s no use speaking to it,”

126
00:10:33,279 --> 00:10:38,617
she thought, “till its ears have come, or at least one of them.”

127
00:10:38,618 --> 00:10:42,938
In another minute the whole head appeared, and then Alice put down her

128
00:10:42,939 --> 00:10:47,259
flamingo, and began an account of the game, feeling very glad she had someone

129
00:10:47,260 --> 00:10:52,419
to listen to her. The Cat seemed to think there was enough of it now in sight, and

130
00:10:52,420 --> 00:10:54,099
no more of it appeared.

131
00:10:54,100 --> 00:10:59,858
“I don’t think they play at all fairly,” Alice began, in a rather complaining tone,

132
00:10:59,859 --> 00:11:04,959
“and they all quarrel so dreadfully one can’t hear oneself speak— —and they don’t

133
00:11:04,960 --> 00:11:09,697
seem to have any rules in particular; at least, if there are, nobody attends to

134
00:11:09,698 --> 00:11:14,978
them— —and you’ve no idea how confusing it is all the things being alive; for

135
00:11:14,979 --> 00:11:19,237
instance, there’s the arch I’ve got to go through next walking about at the other

136
00:11:19,238 --> 00:11:23,019
end of the ground— —and I should have croqueted the Queen’s hedgehog just now,

137
00:11:23,020 --> 00:11:28,599
only it ran away when it saw mine coming!” “How do you like the Queen?” said the Cat

138
00:11:28,600 --> 00:11:35,078
in a low voice. “Not at all,” said Alice: “she’s so extremely—” Just then she noticed

139
00:11:35,079 --> 00:11:40,019
that the Queen was close behind her, listening: so she went on,

140
00:11:40,020 --> 00:11:44,799
“—likely to win, that it’s hardly worthwhile finishing the game.”

141
00:11:44,800 --> 00:11:49,178
The Queen smiled and passed on. “Who are you talking to?”

142
00:11:49,179 --> 00:11:53,799
said the King, going up to Alice, and looking at the Cat’s head with great curiosity.

143
00:11:53,800 --> 00:11:59,559
“It’s a friend of mine— —a shashar capped said Alice: “allow me to introduce it.”

144
00:11:59,560 --> 00:12:03,278
“I don’t like the look of it at all,” said the King:

145
00:12:03,279 --> 00:12:07,718
“however, it may kiss my hand if it likes.” “I’d rather not,”

146
00:12:07,719 --> 00:12:14,019
the Cat remarked. “Don’t be impertinent,” said the King, “and don’t look at me like

147
00:12:14,020 --> 00:12:17,499
that!” He got behind Alice as he spoke. “A

148
00:12:17,500 --> 00:12:20,138
cat may look at a king,” said Alice. “I’ve

149
00:12:20,139 --> 00:12:23,259
read that in some book, but I don’t remember where

150
00:12:23,260 --> 00:12:29,259
it must be removed,” said the King very decidedly, and he called the Queen, who

151
00:12:29,260 --> 00:12:34,178
was passing at the moment, “My dear! I wish you would have this cat removed!”

152
00:12:34,179 --> 00:12:40,419
The Queen had only one way of settling all difficulties, great or small. “Off with

153
00:12:40,420 --> 00:12:43,898
his head!” she said, without even looking round.

154
00:12:43,899 --> 00:12:49,839
“I’ll fetch the executioner myself,” said the King eagerly, and he hurried off.

155
00:12:49,840 --> 00:12:54,459
Alice thought she might as well go back, and see how the game was going on, as she

156
00:12:54,460 --> 00:12:58,599
heard the Queen’s voice in the distance, screaming with passion. She had already

157
00:12:58,600 --> 00:13:02,617
heard her sentence three of the players to be executed for having missed their

158
00:13:02,618 --> 00:13:06,638
turns, and she did not like the look of things at all, as the game was in such

159
00:13:06,639 --> 00:13:11,197
confusion that she never knew whether it was her turn or not. So she went on in

160
00:13:11,198 --> 00:13:13,299
search of her hedgehog.

161
00:13:13,300 --> 00:13:18,099
The hedgehog was engaged in a fight with another hedgehog, which seemed to Alice

162
00:13:18,100 --> 00:13:21,879
an excellent opportunity for croqueting one of them with the other:

163
00:13:21,880 --> 00:13:25,538
the only difficulty was, that her flamingo was gone across to the other

164
00:13:25,539 --> 00:13:29,559
side of the garden, where Alice could see it trying in a helpless sort of way to

165
00:13:29,560 --> 00:13:31,358
fly up into a tree.

166
00:13:31,359 --> 00:13:35,617
By the time she had caught the flamingo and brought it back, the fight was over,

167
00:13:35,618 --> 00:13:41,318
and both the hedgehogs were out of sight: “but it doesn’t matter much,” thought Alice,

168
00:13:41,319 --> 00:13:44,799
“as all the arches are gone from this side of the ground.”

169
00:13:44,800 --> 00:13:49,358
So she tucked it away under her arm, that it might not escape again, and went back

170
00:13:49,359 --> 00:13:52,538
for a little more conversation with her friend.

171
00:13:52,539 --> 00:13:56,858
When she got back to the Cheshire Cat, she was surprised to find quite a large

172
00:13:56,859 --> 00:14:01,959
crowd collected round it: there was a dispute going on between the executioner,

173
00:14:01,960 --> 00:14:07,178
the King, and the Queen, who were all talking at once, while all the rest were

174
00:14:07,179 --> 00:14:11,197
quite silent, and looked very uncomfortable. The moment Alice appeared,

175
00:14:11,198 --> 00:14:15,697
she was appealed to by all there to settle the question, and they repeated

176
00:14:15,698 --> 00:14:20,197
their arguments to her, though, as they all spoke at once, she found it very hard

177
00:14:20,198 --> 00:14:25,059
indeed to make out exactly what they said. The executioner’s argument was, that

178
00:14:25,060 --> 00:14:26,858
you couldn’t cut off a head unless there

179
00:14:26,859 --> 00:14:29,318
was a body to cut it off from: that he

180
00:14:29,319 --> 00:14:30,759
had never had to do such a thing before,

181
00:14:30,760 --> 00:14:34,178
and he wasn’t going to begin at his time of life.

182
00:14:34,179 --> 00:14:38,379
The King’s argument was, that anything that had a head could be beheaded, and

183
00:14:38,380 --> 00:14:41,019
that you weren’t to talk nonsense.

184
00:14:41,020 --> 00:14:44,617
The Queen’s argument was, that if something wasn’t done about it in less

185
00:14:44,618 --> 00:14:48,938
than no time she’d have everybody executed, all round.

186
00:14:48,939 --> 00:14:52,657
(It was this last remark that had made the whole party look so grave and

187
00:14:52,658 --> 00:14:57,157
anxious.) Alice could think of nothing else to say but “It belongs to the

188
00:14:57,158 --> 00:15:01,959
Duchess: you’d better ask her about it.” “She’s in prison,”

189
00:15:01,960 --> 00:15:08,737
the Queen said to the executioner that you're here.” And the executioner went off like an arrow.

190
00:15:08,738 --> 00:15:13,237
The Cat’s head began fading away the moment he was gone, and, by the time he

191
00:15:13,238 --> 00:15:17,858
had come back with the Duchess, it had entirely disappeared; so the King in the

192
00:15:17,859 --> 00:15:22,419
executioner ran wildly up and down looking for it, while the rest of the

193
00:15:22,420 --> 00:15:25,000
party went back to the game
